            <ArticleTitle>Compressive Neuropathy of the Ulnar Nerve: A Perspective on History and Current Controversies.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>464-469</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0363-5023(17)30507-5</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jhsa.2017.03.027</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The untoward effects resulting from compression of the ulnar nerve have been recognized for almost 2 centuries. Initial treatment of cubital tunnel syndrome focused on complete transection of the nerve at the level of the elbow, resulting in initial alleviation of pain but significant functional morbidity. A number of subsequent techniques have been described including in situ decompression, subcutaneous transposition, submuscular transposition, and most recently, endoscopic release. This manuscript focuses on the historical aspects of each of these treatments and our current understanding of their efficacy.</AbstractText>
